.TH ed "" "Command"
.SH NAME
ed \- interactive text editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBed [\-] [\-x] [+cmopsv] [\fIfile\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
ed -- interactive text editor
Usage:	ed [ - ] [ -x ] [ +cmopsv ] [ file ]
Options:
	-	Suppress character counts on r, w, e commands
	-x	Encrypt file
	+c	Print character counts on r, w, e
	+m	Allow multiple commands per line
	+o	Print line counts instead of character counts
	+p	Prompt for each command with *
	+s	Lower case matches upper in patterns
	+v	Verbose error messages
.HE
.B ed
is a line-oriented text editor.
It reads commands from the standard input, normally one command per line,
and edits a copy of the text held in memory; no change reaches a file
until a
.B w
command writes it out.
If the optional
.I file
argument is given,
.B ed
reads it in as though by the
.B e
command below.
.PP
.B ed
keeps a
.I "current line" ,
initially the last line read in and thereafter the line most recently
addressed or affected by a command, and a
.I "current file name" ,
last set by an
.B e ,
.B E ,
or
.B f
command.
.SH "Line Addresses"
Commands may be preceded by zero, one, or two addresses, separated by
a comma or a semicolon.
A semicolon behaves like a comma except that it also sets the current
line to the first address before the second is evaluated.
.IP \fIn\fR
Line number
.IR n ,
counting from 1.
.IP \fB.\fR
The current line.
.IP \fB$\fR
The last line of the text.
.IP "\fB+\fIn\fR, \fB\-\fIn\fR, \fB^\fIn\fR"
An address relative to the current line;
.B ^
is a synonym for
.BR \- .
A lone
.B +
or
.B \-
(or a run of them) adds or subtracts one for each occurrence.
.IP \fB/\fIpattern\fB/\fR
The next line, searching forward and wrapping around the end of the
text, that contains
.IR pattern .
.IP \fB?\fIpattern\fB?\fR
The next line, searching backward and wrapping around the start of
the text, that contains
.IR pattern .
.IP "mark \fIx\fR"
Written as a quote character followed by the lower-case letter
.IR x ,
this addresses the line most recently marked with that letter by the
.B k
command.
.IP \fB&\fR
With no address before it, requests that the following command with
no explicit address print the next 22 lines (a
.IR "page" )
starting at the current line, instead of just one line.
.SH Commands
Each command is a single letter, optionally preceded by the addresses
above.
.IP \fB.\fR 0.6i
With no command letter and no address, print the line after the
current line.
With one address and no command, print that line and make it current.
.IP \fB=\fR
Print the line number of the given address (default: the last line).
.IP "\fB!\fIcommand\fR"
Pass the rest of the input line to the shell for execution.
.IP "[\fIn\fB]a\fR"
Append text, read line by line from the standard input and terminated
by a line containing only
.BR . ,
after line
.IR n .
.IP "[\fIn\fB[,\fIm\fB]c\fR"
Delete the given lines and append new text in their place, as for
.BR a .
.IP "[\fIn\fB[,\fIm\fB]d\fR"
Delete the given lines.
.IP "\fBe \fR[\fIfile\fB]\fR"
Read
.I file
(default: the current file name) into a fresh buffer, discarding the
current text.
.B ed
refuses if there are unsaved changes; repeating the command forces it.
.IP "\fBE \fR[\fIfile\fB]\fR"
Like
.BR e ,
but does not check for unsaved changes.
.IP "\fBf \fR[\fIfile\fB]\fR"
Set the current file name to
.I file
and print it; with no argument, just print the current file name.
.IP "[\fIn\fB[,\fIm\fB]\fBg/\fIpattern\fB/\fIcommands\fR"
For each line in the given range (default: the whole text) that
matches
.I pattern
(default: the last pattern used), execute
.I commands
against it.
.IP "[\fIn\fB]i\fR"
Insert text, as for
.BR a ,
before line
.IR n .
.IP "[\fIn\fB[,\fIm\fB]j\fR"
Join the given lines (default: the current and next line) into one.
.IP "[\fIn\fB]k\fIx\fR"
Mark line
.I n
with the lower-case letter
.IR x ,
for later use as address
.BI ' x.
.IP "[\fIn\fB[,\fIm\fB]l\fR"
Print the given lines, making non-graphic characters visible.
.IP "[\fIn\fB[,\fIm\fB]m\fId\fR"
Move the given lines to follow line
.IR d .
.IP "\fBo \fIoptions\fR"
Change the options in force, as described below; with no
.I options
argument, print the current settings.
.IP "[\fIn\fB[,\fIm\fB]\fBp\fR"
Print the given lines (default: the current line) and make the last
one current.
.IP \fBq\fR
Quit. Refuses if there are unsaved changes; repeating the command
forces it.
.IP \fBQ\fR
Quit unconditionally, discarding unsaved changes.
.IP "[\fIn\fB]r \fR[\fIfile\fB]\fR"
Read
.I file
(default: the current file name) into the text after line
.I n
(default: the last line).
.IP "[\fIn\fB[,\fIm\fB]s/\fIpat\fB/\fIrepl\fB/\fR[\fIg\fB]\fR"
Search each of the given lines for
.I pat
and replace the first occurrence (or, with the trailing
.BR g ,
every occurrence) with
.IR repl .
.IP "[\fIn\fB[,\fIm\fB]t\fId\fR"
Copy the given lines to follow line
.IR d .
.IP "[\fIn\fB]u\fR"
Undo the most recent substitution on line
.IR n .
.IP "[\fIn\fB[,\fIm\fB]v/\fIpattern\fB/\fIcommands\fR"
Like
.BR g ,
but selects lines that do
.I not
match
.IR pattern .
.IP "[\fIn\fB[,\fIm\fB]w \fR[\fIfile\fB]\fR"
Write the given lines (default: the whole text) to
.I file
(default: the current file name), replacing any previous contents.
.IP "[\fIn\fB[,\fIm\fB]W \fR[\fIfile\fB]\fR"
Like
.BR w ,
but appends to
.I file
instead of replacing it.
.IP \fBx\fR
Prompt for a new encryption key for subsequent reads and writes of
the current file.
.PP
After
.BR a ,
.BR c ,
.BR i ,
.BR j ,
.BR s ,
or
.BR u ,
a trailing
.B p
or
.B l
on the same command line prints or lists the resulting current line.
An
.B a ,
.BR c ,
or
.B i
command may be followed by
.BR q ,
which quits after the append/change/insert completes.
.SH Patterns
Search addresses and the
.B s
command take a
.IR pattern ,
built from the following:
.IP \fB^\fR
Anchors the match to the start of the line.
.IP \fB$\fR
Anchors the match to the end of the line.
.IP \fB.\fR
Matches any single character.
.IP \fB*\fR
Matches zero or more repetitions of the preceding character.
.IP \fB[\fIchars\fB]\fR
Matches any one of the enclosed characters; a leading
.B ^
complements the set.
Ranges may be written with
.BR \- .
.IP \fB\e\fIc\fR
Matches the character
.I c
literally, removing any special meaning it would otherwise have.
.IP "\fB\e(\fIpattern\fB\e)\fR"
Delimits a subexpression for use with
.B &
and
.B \e\fId\fR
in the replacement text of an
.B s
command:
.B &
stands for the whole match, and
.B \e\fId\fR
for the text matched by the
.IR d th
.BR \e( ... \e)
pair.
.SH Options
The
.I options
recognized by the
.B o
command, on the command line with a leading
.BR + ,
and in the environment variable
.B ED
are:
.IP \fBc\fR 0.4i
Print character counts after
.BR e ,
.BR r ,
and
.B w
commands (set by default).
.IP \fBm\fR
Allow more than one command on a line.
.IP \fBo\fR
Print line counts, rather than character counts, after
.BR e ,
.BR r ,
and
.B w
commands.
.IP \fBp\fR
Prompt with
.B *
before reading each command.
.IP \fBs\fR
Make lower-case letters in a pattern match either case in the text.
.IP \fBv\fR
Print a fuller message after an error, rather than just
.BR ? .
.PP
Preceding a letter with
.B \-
resets the option instead of setting it.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 12n
\fB/tmp\fR	temporary copy of the edited text
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR sed ,
.B split
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B ed
normally reports an error by printing
.B ?
alone; with the
.B v
option in force it also prints a short explanation.
.SH NOTES
The
.B \-t
command-line option, used to suspend interrupt and quit handling for
a screen editor built on top of
.BR ed ,
and the
.B \-u
option, which disables output buffering, are not part of the usual
interactive interface and are not listed above.
